\chapter{Tutorial: Assignments and grading}
\label{tutorial-assignments-and-grading}

[[assignments-and-grading]] continues where [[getting-started]]
(\cref{tutorial-getting-started}) stopped: the learner can select a
course with [[-c]] and knows that the output is tab-separated text.
Its object of learning is the path from an assignment to a grade: which
assignments a course has, what students submitted, what grades they
have, and, finally, how a grade is set from the command line.

The critical aspects are these:
\begin{description}
\item[List versus view] [[list]] prints one row per item; [[view]]
  prints the content of the items.
  The same distinction holds for assignments and submissions, and it
  is met twice so that it is seen to be a pattern rather than a fact
  about one verb.
\item[Narrowing] [[-a]] selects assignments and [[-u]] selects users,
  by regex, exactly as [[-c]] selects courses.
\item[Reading versus writing] Everything up to [[results]] only reads
  from Canvas.
  [[grade]] writes, and the tutorial treats that boundary as the most
  important thing it teaches.
\end{description}

The arc is:
\begin{enumerate}
\item list and view assignments (steps 1--2), then narrow the list to
  the ones with ungraded work (step~3);
\item list and view submissions, adding [[-a]] and [[-u]] (steps 4--5);
\item export the results (step~6);
\item read the warning that [[grade]] prints (step~7), compose a
  grading command without running it (step~8), and name the commands
  that write (step~9).
\end{enumerate}

\section{Why nothing here changes a grade}

A tutorial that had the learner set a real grade would need a course
where a wrong grade does no harm, and the tutorial cannot know whether
the learner has one.
The grading half of the tutorial therefore never runs [[grade]].
The learner first reads the command's own warning, then writes the
command as an answer to a question, which the tutorial checks for the
options that make a grading command safe: an anchored course, an
anchored assignment and an anchored user.
That is the shape they should type when they do it for real, and they
will have typed it once already.

As in the previous tutorial, shell steps validate the typed command
with a regex anchored on [[canvaslms]] and the verb, since the output
depends on the learner's course.
The one output match is the warning in step~7, which is printed by
[[argparse]] and identical everywhere.
No step uses shell hooks.

