\section{Step 1: List the assignments of a course}

The first step transfers the [[-c]] option from [[users]] to a new
verb.
The pattern requires the [[list]] verb explicitly, although
[[assignments]] alone would do the same, because the next step
contrasts [[list]] with [[view]] and that contrast needs both words on
the screen.

<<list assignments step>>=
# List the assignments of a course

```tutorial-step
required_patterns:
  - mode: regex
    pattern: (?:^|\s)canvaslms\s+assignments\s+list\b
  - mode: regex
    pattern: (?:^|\s)canvaslms\s+assignments\s+list[^\n]*\s-c\b
hint: "The assignments verb has verbs of its own. Use list, and select the course with -c as you did for users."
```

Run `canvaslms assignments list -c "<regex>"`, where `<regex>` selects
one of your courses as in the previous tutorial.

Each row is one assignment: the course, the assignment group, the
name, and the due, unlock and lock dates, separated by tabs. Note the
names; the next steps select one of them by regex.

Exit the shell when you have the list.
@

\section{Step 2: Read one assignment}

Only the verb changes: [[list]] becomes [[view]], and [[-a]] narrows
the result to one assignment.
Without [[-a]], [[view]] would print every assignment's description,
which is a wall of text rather than a contrast; with it, the learner
sees the one description that belongs to a row they just saw.
The pattern requires [[-a]] for that reason.

<<view an assignment step>>=
# Read one assignment

```tutorial-step
required_patterns:
  - mode: regex
    pattern: (?:^|\s)canvaslms\s+assignments\s+view\b
  - mode: regex
    pattern: (?:^|\s)canvaslms\s+assignments\s+view[^\n]*\s-a\b
hint: "Same command as before with view instead of list, plus -a and a regex that matches one assignment name."
```

Run `canvaslms assignments view -c "<regex>" -a "<assignment>"`, where
`<assignment>` is a regex matching one of the names from step 1.

Where `list` printed one row per assignment, `view` prints the
assignment itself: its name, dates and description, converted from
Canvas's HTML to Markdown. The `-a` option narrows the result the same
way `-c` does, by a regex on the name.

Exit the shell when you have read the description.
@

\section{Step 3: Narrow to the ungraded ones}

Back to [[list]], with a flag instead of a regex.
[[-U]] keeps only assignments that have submissions waiting for a
grade, which is the question a teacher asks most days, and it is the
first option in these tutorials that filters on state rather than on a
name.
A learner whose course has nothing ungraded gets an empty list; the
step says so, because an empty result is the correct answer there and
must not look like a failure.

<<ungraded assignments step>>=
# Narrow to the ungraded ones

```tutorial-step
required_patterns:
  - mode: regex
    pattern: (?:^|\s)canvaslms\s+assignments\s+list[^\n]*\s-U\b
hint: "Back to list, with the option that keeps only assignments with ungraded submissions. Its help text names it."
```

Run `canvaslms assignments list -c "<regex>" -U`.

The `-U` option keeps only assignments that have submissions nobody
has graded yet. Unlike `-a`, it filters on the state of the assignment
rather than on its name. If your course has no ungraded work the list
is empty, and that is the right answer.

Exit the shell when you have the (possibly empty) list.
@

\section{Step 4: List the submissions}

The list-and-view pattern is now repeated on a second noun.
[[submissions list]] takes the same [[-c]] and [[-a]] as the
assignment verbs, so the learner types a command they already know
with one word changed, and sees one row per student instead of one row
per assignment.
The pattern requires [[-a]]: without it, [[submissions list]] prints
every submission of every assignment, which is slow on a large course
and teaches nothing the narrowed list does not.

<<list submissions step>>=
# List the submissions

```tutorial-step
required_patterns:
  - mode: regex
    pattern: (?:^|\s)canvaslms\s+submissions\s+list\b
  - mode: regex
    pattern: (?:^|\s)canvaslms\s+submissions\s+list[^\n]*\s-a\b
hint: "Change the noun: submissions list takes the same -c and -a as assignments list."
```

Run `canvaslms submissions list -c "<regex>" -a "<assignment>"`, with
the same assignment regex as in step 2.

Each row is one student's submission to that assignment: the course,
the assignment, the student, the grade if there is one, and the dates
of submission and grading. The `-c` and `-a` options are the same as
before; only the noun changed.

Exit the shell when you have the list.
@

\section{Step 5: Read one student's submission}

As step~2 did for assignments, this step contrasts [[view]] with
[[list]] on submissions, and adds the third selector, [[-u]].
The pattern requires [[-u]] so that the learner reads one submission
rather than all of them; [[submissions view]] downloads attachments,
and one student's are enough to see what it does.

<<view a submission step>>=
# Read one student's submission

```tutorial-step
required_patterns:
  - mode: regex
    pattern: (?:^|\s)canvaslms\s+submissions\s+view\b
  - mode: regex
    pattern: (?:^|\s)canvaslms\s+submissions\s+view[^\n]*\s-u\b
hint: "view instead of list again, plus -u with a regex that matches one student from the list."
```

Pick one student from the list in step 4 and run
`canvaslms submissions view -c "<regex>" -a "<assignment>" -u "<user>"`,
where `<user>` is a regex matching that student's name or login.

`view` prints the submission itself: the text the student wrote, the
comments, and the contents of any attached files. The `-u` option is
the third selector you have met, and it works like `-c` and `-a`: a
regex, matched against the student.

Exit the shell when you have read the submission.
@

\section{Step 6: Export the results}

[[results]] is the reading command a course ends with: one row per
student and assignment with the grade and its date, in the form the
[[ladok]] tool imports.
The step pipes it into [[head]] so the learner sees the format without
scrolling through a whole course, which also repeats the pipe from the
first tutorial in a new setting.
The pattern only requires [[results]] with [[-c]]; the pipe is
suggested, not enforced.

<<export results step>>=
# Export the results

```tutorial-step
required_patterns:
  - mode: regex
    pattern: (?:^|\s)canvaslms\s+results\b[^\n]*\s-c\b
hint: "results takes -c like the others. Pipe it into head to see only the first rows."
```

Run `canvaslms results -c "<regex>" | head`.

`results` prints one row per student and graded assignment: the
course, the assignment, the student, the grade and the date it was
set. This is the format other tools import, for instance to report
grades to a student registry, and `head` shows only the first rows so
you can see the shape without the whole course.

Exit the shell when you have seen the rows.
@

\section{Step 7: Read the warning before you grade}

This is the try-first moment of the tutorial: before the learner
composes a grading command, they meet the command's own warning.
[[submissions grade -h]] says \emph{Hic sunt dracones} and explains
why: the same regex selectors that made reading convenient will grade
every submission they match.
Reading that in the tool's words, before any grading command is
written, is worth more than the tutorial paraphrasing it.
The warning is printed by [[argparse]] and is the same everywhere, so
this is the one step here that validates output.

<<read the grading warning step>>=
# Read the warning before you grade

```tutorial-step
required_patterns:
  - mode: regex
    pattern: (?:^|\s)canvaslms\s+submissions\s+grade\s+(?:-h|--help)\b
  - Hic sunt dracones
hint: "Ask the grade verb of submissions for its help. Read the description line."
```

Run `canvaslms submissions grade -h`.

Read the description. The `grade` verb takes the same `-c`, `-a` and
`-u` selectors as `list` and `view`, and it sets the grade given by
`-g` on **every submission the selectors match**. A regex that was
convenient for reading, matching several assignments or several
students, grades all of them. That is what the dragons are about.

Exit the shell when you have read the help.
@

\section{Step 8: Write the grading command (do not run it)}

The learner now composes the command as an answer, and the check
demands the shape that keeps the dragons away: a course, an assignment
and a user selector, all present, and a grade.
Lookaheads let the options appear in any order.
The instructions ask for anchored regexes, [[^...$]], so that each
selector matches exactly one thing; the check cannot verify the
anchors without knowing the learner's names, but the learner has now
typed the safe form once.
The optional host prefix in the pattern accepts [[poetry run]] as the
shell steps do.

<<compose a grading command step>>=
# Write the grading command (do not run it)

```tutorial-step
kind: input
answers:
  - mode: regex
    pattern: ^(\S+\s+)?canvaslms\s+(?:submissions\s+)?grade\b(?=[^\n]*\s-c\b)(?=[^\n]*\s-a\b)(?=[^\n]*\s-u\b)(?=[^\n]*\s-g\b)
hint: "One line: canvaslms submissions grade with -c, -a, -u and -g, each selector anchored with ^ and $ so it matches exactly one thing."
```

Type, without running it, the command that would give one student a
grade on one assignment: `canvaslms submissions grade` with `-c` for
the course, `-a` for the assignment, `-u` for the student and `-g` for
the grade. Anchor each regex with `^` and `$` so that it matches
exactly one course, one assignment and one student, for example
`canvaslms submissions grade -c "^DD1301$" -a "^Lab 1$" -u "^alice$" -g P`.

You may add `-m "<message>"` to leave a comment for the student. When
you grade for real, run the same selectors with `submissions list`
first and check that exactly the rows you mean are printed.
@

\section{Step 9: Which of these write to Canvas?}

The closing question makes the reading-versus-writing boundary
explicit.
Four of the five options are commands the learner has run in this
tutorial and one, [[assignments set-dates]], they have not; it is
included so that the answer is a judgement about names, not a recall
of what was typed.
Both writing commands take the same selectors as the reading ones,
which is exactly why the boundary has to be learned rather than
noticed.

<<name the writing commands step>>=
# Which of these write to Canvas?

```tutorial-step
kind: multi_select
options:
  - assignments list
  - submissions view
  - submissions grade
  - results
  - assignments set-dates
answers:
  - submissions grade
  - assignments set-dates
hint: "Two of them change something in Canvas; the other three only read. Their names say which is which."
```

Which of these commands change something in Canvas?

Every command in this tutorial took the same selectors, so nothing in
the shape of a command tells you whether it reads or writes; only the
verb does. Before you run a writing verb, run the reading verb with
the same selectors and check what it matches.
@
